# Test guide: `{{ project_name }}`

Rules for the tests in this directory. An agent reads this file
whenever it works in `tests/`; the project-wide rules are in the root
`AGENTS.md`.

## Layout

- Tests live in `tests/` and may use bare `assert` (ruff `S101` is
  waived there).
- Mirror the package layout under `tests/`, one `test_<module>.py` per
  source module: `{{ package_name }}/sub/mod.py` is tested by
  `tests/sub/test_mod.py`. Keep `__init__.py` markers in test
  subpackages (matches the `INP` ruff rule).
- Not every source file needs a dedicated test file: types-only
  modules, re-export `__init__.py` markers, and details covered through
  a public-facing module are intentional exceptions.
- Fixtures live in `tests/conftest.py` when you need them (modern
  default, applies to `tests/` only). Use a root `conftest.py` only for
  `pytest_plugins` declarations, doctest fixtures shared with source
  files, or project-wide collection hooks.
- Code shared across tests lives in a `fixtures/` package: either
  `tests/fixtures/`, whose modules the root `conftest.py` loads through
  `pytest_plugins`, or `tests/<sub>/fixtures/` for one subpackage,
  imported by that subpackage's `conftest.py` so it applies below it
  only (pytest honors `pytest_plugins` in the root `conftest.py`
  alone).
- Add a `conftest.py` or a `helpers.py` wherever tests need one:
  per-package configuration in `conftest.py`, plain helper functions
  and classes in `tests/<pkg>/helpers.py`. Shared skip marks go in
  `tests/marks.py`.
- Import the code under test the way its users do, from its public
  path. Import a helper by its absolute path,
  `from tests.<pkg>.helpers import ...`: `tests/` is a package, so this
  works under `--import-mode=importlib`.
- Type annotations are optional in tests: `ruff`'s `ANN` rules are
  waived for `tests/**`, and `ty` does not check `tests/`.

## Test data

- Data files the tests read live in `tests/assets/`, which is not a
  Python package and mirrors nothing. If the project has a module named
  `assets`, choose another name and record it below the marker in the
  root `AGENTS.md`.
- A test that writes files does so under pytest's `tmp_path` (or
  `tmp_path_factory` for data shared by a module), never in the
  working directory or a fixed path.
- Prefer data a test builds in `tmp_path` over a committed file. Commit
  a file only when building it would hide what the test checks: a real
  file header, a sample from a device.
- Data too large or private to commit goes in `tests/assets/local/`,
  which `.gitignore` excludes, and is opt-in: its fixture is
  parametrized over the files present, so a clone without them skips
  those tests instead of failing.

## Writing tests

- Flat module-level `def test_*` functions by default; reach for a
  plain `class TestX:` (grouping only, no inheritance) to organize a
  large or multi-feature surface. Don't mix the two styles within one
  file.
- Name a test for the behavior it checks, as a phrase:
  `test_folder_error_ends_with_the_offending_full_path`.
- Add no docstrings or comments to test functions and classes; the
  name says what a test checks. Keep the comments already there, and
  document an existing test only when the user asks for it. A helper,
  fixture, or fake that several tests share may take a short
  docstring.
- When a parametrized case's values do not say what it covers, name
  it with `ids=` or `pytest.param(..., id="empty-source")`, so a
  failure reads `test_load[empty-source]` rather than
  `test_load[3-True]`.
- Tests are independent of each other and of their order. A test that
  changes module-level state (a registry, a cache, an environment
  variable) restores it: `monkeypatch` for an attribute or a variable,
  an autouse fixture in that package's `conftest.py` for state a whole
  package touches. Fixtures keep the default function scope; widen it
  only for setup that is expensive and never mutated.
- Seed randomness per test with a local generator
  (`random.Random(<seed>)`, `numpy.random.default_rng(<seed>)`), never a
  global seed. Compare floats with `pytest.approx`, or
  `numpy.testing.assert_allclose` for arrays, giving the tolerance the
  check needs rather than the default.
- Test through the public API. Reach into a private name (`_name`) only
  when it is itself the contract under test, with `# noqa: SLF001` on
  that line.
- Tests never touch the network or an outside service: fake the
  boundary with `monkeypatch`.
- Assertions check concrete return values *and* side effects, not
  merely "no exception raised"; error paths use
  `pytest.raises(..., match=...)`; verify numbers against independently
  computed values, not the implementation's own output; make timing and
  IO deterministic (an injected clock, fault injection) rather than
  flaky. A good test fails on *subtle* breakage, not just obvious
  breakage. When a contract is "one batched call per source",
  verify it with a spy, not only by the result.
- A warning the code emits on purpose is behavior: assert it with
  `pytest.warns(Category, match=...)`. Test log output where it is the
  product (a run's report, a message a user acts on) through
  `caplog.records`: the level and `record.getMessage()`, not the
  formatted `caplog.text`.
- Replace or observe a collaborator with `monkeypatch`, not
  `unittest.mock`: patch the attribute with a small fake, or wrap the
  real method in a spy that records each call and delegates to it. A
  spy keeps the real behavior, so the test checks the contract rather
  than a scripted double. Patch the name where the code looks it up; a
  fixture that patches returns what it records.
- A bug fix starts with a test that fails on the bug. Fix the code and
  keep the test as the guard, named for the behavior it pins down, not
  for the bug report.

## Skipped tests

- A test that cannot run everywhere is skipped (never deleted or left
  failing), and its `reason` names what is missing ("needs a CUDA
  device", "symlinks need privilege on Windows"). Pick the mechanism by
  what is missing:
  - an optional dependency: `pytest.importorskip("<module>")` at the
    top of the module;
  - a platform, device, or service: a `skipif` mark defined once in
    `tests/marks.py` and imported where needed
    (`from tests.marks import requires_cuda`), never redefined per
    file;
  - data the repository does not hold: the opt-in fixture of
    *Test data*.
- Parametrize over the condition rather than duplicating a test
  (`pytest.param("cuda", marks=requires_cuda)`), so a machine without
  it still runs the other cases.
- When CI cannot run the tests (hardware its runners lack), say so
  below the marker in the root `AGENTS.md`. The local run is then the
  gate: run the full suite on a machine that has the hardware before
  asking for a commit, and report any group that skipped.

## Coverage and speed

- With `fail_under = 0` coverage is measured, not enforced. Once the
  tests cover the {% if is_library %}public API{% else %}main code paths{% endif %}, set it a few points below the
  measured total and raise it as coverage grows; the libraries built
  from this template hold 95.
- Never lower `fail_under` to let a change pass: cover the change, or
  exclude a line that cannot run under test with `# pragma: no cover`
  and a comment saying why. Leave out only modules with nothing to
  execute, such as type aliases, through `omit` in
  `[tool.coverage.run]`.
- Keep the suite fast enough to run before every commit. A test that
  takes seconds gets `@pytest.mark.slow`, registered in
  `pyproject.toml`; `uv run pytest -m "not slow"` serves quick
  iteration, and the full suite still runs before a commit and in CI.
